xtra light just really affects the overall image and washes it out. It's like trying to watch a movie on a movie screen where, you know, you have the lights on in your room, and it's just your screen seems dim and not sharp. And then you turn off the lights in the room, and all of a sudden your TV or your, you know, movie screen looks a lot crisper and clearer, and the colors look really good. It's kind of that same thing. You're making sure all that ambient light that's bouncing around in there, that you get that out of the overall image. I was laying beside a friend one time. It was his shot on a mule deer we'd found, and we were looking into the sun. I was laying there looking through my scope. I had a tag to it. I was going to back him up if for whatever reason he couldn't get it, right? And I'm like, come on. We need to get going on this, right? He's like, I can't see. I cannot see through my scope. He was using a different brand. I'm not going to throw anybody under the bus, but it was typically considered a pretty good scope, right? And I said, what do you mean you can't see? You got something in your eye or something snarky like that? And he's like, no, I can't see through my scope. The sun's glare is just, I can't see through it. I said, hang on. And I leaned over and looked through his scope. I thought maybe he had something weird going on. Sure enough, just this like yellow haze, and you couldn't see through it. And I just rolled over and said, get behind my gun. He got behind me. He's like, whoa, bang. That's right, yep. Yeah, sometimes it can be I can't see a thing. Sometimes it looks like it's still a deer there, but I can't really make a good placement. I can't make a good shot, right? And kind of anywhere in between, just depending on those lighting conditions. The deer never come out when the lighting conditions are perfect. I've learned that over many years of hunting. It's always right at that beginning light or last light. They'll just put themselves between you and the sun. I don't know how they figure that out, but they always seem to do that. I think that's an instinct they get with their mama's milk, you know. That's right. Absolutely. Kind of funny. Teach us a little bit about coatings and how those actually affect. And even if it's just, you know, keeping it simple, number of layers and how many surfaces are coated. Because I know a lot of companies don't coat all the surfaces. Absolutely, yeah. Yeah, a lot of, you know, just about anybody will coat maybe the outer part, you know, of your objective and then maybe the eyepiece. Because those are definitely really important ones to coat. We fully multi-coat each side of every lens. They may be different coatings because they're doing different pieces of work there. But you're really making coatings because you're wanting a really good transmission of light. So, you know, light needs to move through air into different types of glass, back into air, back into glass. And it needs to be able to handle all those transitions really efficiently. And that's ultimately what coatings do is they make sure that that light beam coming through can enter into the glass and exit that glass really efficiently. And so you get an overall light transmission by optimizing your coatings. But there's a lot of other coatings as well. So there's ones that are anti-reflective. So you don't want, you know, some of your light coming in and then, you know, just a small fraction of that light. You know, any light that doesn't go through, it had to go somewhere. So it either stopped and didn't transmit through that material or a lot of times it'll bounce off and then come back the other way and maybe go up to the side or back out the front or, you know, it just sort of is reflected. So coatings that handle those reflections come to kind of glare performance, but overall transmission as well, clarity of the image. Little visual aid right here for the people watching on YouTube. Just look at my glasses as I move my head around. That's right. See how those lights are reflecting off my glasses? They won't do that on blue balls. That's exactly it, right? Is, you know, and sometimes that can be different colors too, right? I could see a little bit of color in that reflection. It wasn't a pure sort of white light. So the different colors interact differently through those transitions. And so, and then you have some ones in the outer too. So things that are hydrophobic coatings and other things that really help hard coatings so you don't scratch the external part of your lens. And then the hydrophobic to be able to shed water and dust and debris, make it easier to clean. And so, yeah, quite a bit of coating technology. And like you said, layers, right? A lot of these deposited materials that go onto the glass are done in these big vacuum chambers. And they're really alternating. They're putting one material down teeny tiny, like smaller than a virus type of level thickness. Just a few nanometers of thickness. And then they're alternating. And then they may be capped on either end. And there'll be other processes to make sure that those materials bond and adhere to the glass and are really uniform across a curved surface. As you can imagine, if you're coating something like a table that's nice and flat, you can probably get a nice even coat. But if you're coating a curved lens, it can be really hard to get an even coating throughout. And that really matters. And so, generally, everybody has the same access to the same exact glass, the same exact coating technologies. There's not a lot of secret sauce there. There is in how you construct that. And then there's also just the cost of doing that. So a lot of people, hey, I'm making a lower cost scope or I'm trying to hit a lower price point. And I'll sacrifice some performance by not coating some of those lenses. Those are some of the tradeoffs that you get in other brands. But, you know, in our coatings, we're really making sure that both sides of those lenses are coated. And it's to be able to maximize that light coming through and then make sure that those reflections and everything like we just saw with your glasses, right, are minimized in the scope. So you get the best image overall. Yeah, very cool. You hear people, especially when they're using spotting scopes and binoculars and so forth, talking about how a certain type of optic will make the colors of game, whether it's deer, moose or bear or whatever, stand out from among the vegetation or the trees or the shadows or what have you, where they're standing. Some of that is engineered by the type of colors that these coatings control. Is that correct? Absolutely, yeah. Yeah, the coatings, the glass itself too, right, play all a role in, you know, the color of light that is coming through the optic and what, you know, ends up at the back end. So basically what you're seeing there is, hey, all the normal colors came from the environment. They came into the scope. And if you're seeing a different color than what you see with your normal eye, that just means some of those colors didn't come all the way through. Also, you can have colors that come through that they actually don't come back into focus. And so you'll see a, you may see a focused image, but that's what you see when, you know, there's technical terms that we use, chromatic aberration and those things. But what that ultimately means is you're not actually focusing the colors back into the same spot. And if people have ever looked at, you know, a light target or a dark target and they're next to one another, like just a white or a black, and you see a little bit of color like blue over on the white, and then you see a little bit of color like on that, and you don't get a nice sharp line. That's basically focus. The image isn't focused, but it's not focused for all the colors. That blue color sort of strayed and didn't come back into focus. So it's really